number|title|condition|evidence|remediation|limitation|attack|nist|nist_rationale|sources|severity
001|An AD-controlled application owner can add a privileged workload credential|A source account-control path reaches an application owner AND the owner can effectively add a usable credential AND the corresponding service principal has sensitive application permissions.|Verified user mapping; application and service principal IDs; owner capability; credential restrictions; granted app roles.|Protect workload owners and reduce standing application permissions.|Ownership does not mean all APIs or Microsoft-owned apps are writable. Delegated scopes are not app-only grants.|T1098.001;T1078.004|AC-3;AC-6;IA-5|Restrict who can create workload authenticators and the permissions they unlock.||High
002|A lower-trust host administrator controls a privileged cloud automation identity|An unapproved principal administers an on-premises automation host AND a documented credential or signing service on that host authenticates a workload with sensitive cloud grants.|Host administration path; service configuration metadata; credential reference and use identity; app role grants.|Protect the automation host or move privileged credential use into an independently controlled service.|A client ID in a config file is not a secret. Credential reference alone does not establish local usability.|T1078.004;T1552.001|AC-6;IA-5;SC-7|Align host administration and workload-authenticator protection with the workload's privilege.||High
003|Workload credential storage is readable outside the approved boundary|An unapproved principal has effective file or key-use access to the credential store used by a sensitive hybrid application AND any separate decryption/use boundary is also satisfied.|File/key ACL metadata; credential-store type; process identity; decryption/use authorization; exact cloud application grants.|Restrict credential storage and use, and plan rotation after confirmed unauthorized exposure.|Never read secret values. Encrypted content or public certificate access alone is not sufficient for credential usability.|T1552.001;T1649|AC-3;IA-5;SC-12|Protect stored authenticators and cryptographic key use.||High
004|A controllable workload issuer is trusted by a privileged cloud application|An on-premises issuer or execution environment can produce assertions matching a workload federated identity credential AND the target workload has sensitive permissions.|Issuer identity; exact issuer/subject/audience trust; issuer control path; supported token exchange; workload grants.|Narrow federation subjects and audiences and protect the issuer or runner boundary.|Do not conflate user-domain federation with workload federation. A trusted issuer URL alone proves no ability to mint accepted assertions.|T1484.002;T1078.004|IA-5;AC-3;AC-6|Protect workload authentication trust and constrain accepted assertions.||High
005|A controllable synced user can exercise sensitive delegated application access|A verified source control path reaches a user with sensitive delegated consent AND the application and user permissions jointly authorize the relevant resource operation.|User mapping; delegated grant; resource scopes; user resource permissions; consent and CA context.|Reduce delegated grants and protect the user identity and consent workflow.|Delegated consent is bounded by user privilege and session requirements. Do not treat it as tenant-wide application permission.|T1078.004|AC-3;AC-6|Limit combined user and delegated-application authorization.||High
006|A synced operator can grant privileged application access|An AD-controlled cloud identity has effective authority to grant sensitive app roles or permissions to an application within the assessed scope.|Source control path; directory/API permissions; resource grant-management restrictions; eligible target applications.|Separate privileged consent administration and review the allowed grant scope.|Some permissions require stronger grant authority than application administration. Check the exact resource API and permission type.|T1098.003|AC-3;AC-6;CM-5|Restrict changes that expand workload authorization.||High
007|A retired on-premises integration still has usable cloud privileges|Owner review confirms an integration is retired AND its cloud service principal remains enabled with sensitive grants AND valid credential metadata or a usable federation trust remains.|Retirement record; host/service inventory; principal state; grants; credential validity metadata; trust state.|Remove unused grants and retire the identity through the approved integration lifecycle.|No recent sign-in alone is insufficient. A credential's expiration metadata does not prove compromise or use.|T1078.004|AC-2;AC-6;IA-5|Retire workload identities and authenticators together with their integrations.||High
008|An exposed Exchange hybrid host holds overly broad cloud application authority|A lower-trust principal controls an Exchange hybrid host AND the exact hybrid application's usable credentials and effective cloud permissions exceed the approved integration scope.|Host control; hybrid configuration; dedicated versus legacy app identity; credential-use metadata; current resource grants.|Protect Exchange administration and migrate or narrow hybrid application authority using supported vendor guidance.|Do not assume every legacy application is still active or exploitable. Compare the installed hybrid model and current cloud enforcement.|T1078.004;T1098.001|AC-6;IA-5;CM-6|Constrain the cloud authority carried by an on-premises integration.|MS-EXO-HYBRID|High
009|Cloud management rights permit code execution on an on-premises bridge|A lower-trust cloud principal has an effective supported Arc or equivalent management action capable of privileged execution on a registered on-premises hybrid bridge.|Cloud role/action and resource scope; deny assignments; extension/run-command restrictions; machine registration; host-to-bridge binding.|Restrict cloud execution roles over hybrid identity machines and protect the machine registration boundary.|Azure directory roles are not Azure resource roles. Reader or inventory access does not establish guest execution.|T1072;T1078.004|AC-3;AC-6;CM-5|Constrain cloud management actions that can change on-premises identity infrastructure.||High
010|A cloud runbook editor can execute through a privileged Hybrid Worker|An unapproved principal can change and execute a runbook assigned to an on-premises Hybrid Worker AND the worker's effective identity or host rights reach a hybrid control-plane asset.|Automation account scope; edit and start permissions; runbook/worker binding; worker execution identity; approval boundary; target rights.|Separate runbook editing and execution authority and limit worker privileges and network scope.|Runbook read access or an unassigned worker is not an execution path. Enforced independent approval can break unilateral control.|T1072;T1059.001|AC-5;AC-6;CM-5|Protect the code and execution authority crossing through hybrid automation.||High
